Run files in browsers

Usage: whirl run [OPTIONS] [PATH]...

Arguments:
  [PATH]...
          Files to run; directories recurse to *.whirl

Options:
      --rerun-failed <REPORT>
          Rerun whole failed or errored files from a JSON report

      --browser <NAME>
          Set the `browser` option, as `-O browser=NAME` does

      --headed
          Run with a visible browser window

      --jobs <N>
          Worker slots for parallel files

      --var <k=v>
          Define a variable (repeatable)

      --variables-file <PATH>
          Load variables from a file

  -O <KEY=VALUE>
          Set an option for every file, as in `-O browser=firefox` (repeatable)

      --out <DIR>
          Output directory for screenshots, traces, video, and other evidence
          
          [default: whirl-artifacts]

      --trace
          Record a Playwright trace per file; saved only when the file fails

      --report-junit <PATH>
          Write a JUnit XML report

      --report-json <PATH>
          Write a JSON report

      --report-html <PATH>
          Write a standalone HTML report with embedded recordings and screenshots

      --report-metadata <PATH>
          Read author-written report and flow descriptions from a JSON file

      --fail-fast
          Stop scheduling new files after the first failure

      --update-snapshots
          Write or refresh SNAPSHOT baselines instead of comparing

      --video
          Record a .webm video of each file's run

      --video-fps <N>
          Frames per second for --video on Chromium (1 to 60; default 60)

      --har
          Record a .har network log per file

      --jev
          Plan ACT with TypeSafe Jev first, falling back to the model option

      --cache <MODE>
          What to do with each flow's AI cache

          Possible values:
          - replay: Replay hits; resolve misses with the model; never write
          - update: As `replay`, then write the cache of each file that passed
          - only:   Fail a miss instead of asking the model
          
          [default: replay]

      --load-state <FILE>
          Start each file's browser from this saved state

      --save-state <FILE>
          Write the final browser state after a successful run of one file

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
